(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-cell-physiology-membranes-quiz"]={"title":"Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes — Physiology Exam 1","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the fundamental principle Claude Bernard articulated with the concept of the \"milieu interieur\"?","choices":["Disease is simply the study of disordered body function at the cellular level","Cells reproduce and divide continuously via the process of mitosis","Genes alone control all cellular behavior, independent of the extracellular environment","The function of the whole body is much more than the sum of its parts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Homeostasis is the maintenance of a stable \"milieu interieur,\" and Bernard's fundamental principle is that the function of the whole body is much more than the sum of its parts. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is pathophysiology?","choices":["The study of disordered body function (disease), the basis for clinical medicine","The study of feedback control mechanisms only, within otherwise healthy tissue","The study of genetics and inherited chromosomal disorders exclusively","The study of normal cell organelles and their function in healthy tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pathophysiology is the study of disordered body function (i.e., disease), and it forms the basis for clinical medicine. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Approximately how many cells make up the human body?","choices":["~35 to 40 trillion","~10 million","~100 trillion","~35 to 40 billion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cells are the basic structural and functional unit of the body, numbering approximately 35 to 40 trillion. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"The baroreceptor reflex response to hemorrhage (increased sympathetic activity, increased heart rate, vasoconstriction to restore arterial pressure) is an example of what kind of control?","choices":["Negative feedback, promoting stability","Feed-forward control, anticipating pressure change","Positive feedback, amplifying the pressure change","No feedback control involved whatsoever"],"answer":0,"exp":"The baroreceptor reflex's response to hemorrhage/decreased arterial pressure is negative feedback, which promotes stability by counteracting the change. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Cardiopulmonary reflexes that anticipate an upcoming change in blood pressure (rather than responding after the fact) are examples of what kind of control?","choices":["Positive feedback","Feed-forward control","Negative feedback","Genomic control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Feed-forward control anticipates change, as with cardiopulmonary reflexes, in contrast to feedback control which responds after a change has occurred. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Hemorrhagic shock and the depolarization phase of the action potential are both classic physiological examples of what type of feedback?","choices":["No feedback","Feed-forward control","Negative feedback","Positive feedback"],"answer":3,"exp":"Both hemorrhagic shock (a dangerous, self-amplifying cycle) and the action potential's rapid depolarization are examples of positive feedback, where the response amplifies the initial change. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which of the following is most precisely, tightly controlled in the body?","choices":["Plasma [H+] concentration","Systemic blood flow","Arterial blood pressure","Basal metabolic rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Some variables, like plasma [H+] concentration (~3-5 x 10^-8 moles/L), are precisely controlled, while others like blood pressure, blood flow, and metabolic rate vary more with sex, age, body size, and circadian rhythms. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What percentage of cell mass is typically composed of water?","choices":["2-95%","1-6%","70-85%","10-20%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Water makes up 70-85% of cell mass, with proteins at 10-20%, lipids at 2-95% (highly variable by cell type), and carbohydrates at 1-6%. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What distinguishes rough (granular) ER from smooth ER?","choices":["Rough ER is found only within the nucleus, forming a direct continuation of the nuclear envelope","They are functionally identical structures, both synthesising lipids and processing newly made proteins equally","Rough ER has ribosomes on its outer surface and processes newly synthesized proteins; smooth ER is the site of lipid synthesis","Rough ER is the site of lipid synthesis; smooth ER has ribosomes covering its outer surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rough (granular) ER has ribosomes covering its outer surface and extrudes/processes newly synthesized proteins into its matrix, while smooth ER is the site of lipid synthesis (phospholipids, cholesterol). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What happens to substances after they arrive at the Golgi apparatus from the smooth ER?","choices":["They are used directly to form the new strands of nuclear DNA during cell replication","They are processed (phosphorylated, glycosylated), then concentrated, sorted, and packaged for secretion","They are immediately degraded entirely by lysosomal acid hydrolase enzymes","They are immediately released directly into the nucleus for permanent storage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Golgi apparatus receives transport vesicles from the smooth ER and processes substances (phosphorylation, glycosylation) before concentrating, sorting, and packaging them for secretion. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the difference between constitutive and stimulated secretion?","choices":["Only stimulated secretion involves the Golgi apparatus","They are the same process","Constitutive secretion requires a trigger; stimulated secretion happens randomly","Constitutive secretion happens randomly; stimulated secretion requires a trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Constitutive secretion happens randomly (continuously), while stimulated secretion requires a specific trigger before secretory vesicles release their contents. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do lysosomes contain, and what is their primary function?","choices":["DNA polymerase and helicase enzymes, used for unwinding and then replicating the nuclear DNA","Hydrolytic enzymes (acid hydrolases) such as phosphatases, nucleases, proteases, and lysozymes, for digesting material","Oxidase enzymes exclusively, for detoxifying alcohol and other toxic compounds","Free ribosomes and rRNA, for synthesizing new cytoplasmic proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lysosomes are vesicular organelles formed from budding Golgi that contain hydrolytic acid hydrolases (phosphatases, nucleases, proteases, lipid-degrading enzymes, lysozymes) and fuse with endocytotic vesicles to digest their contents. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How do peroxisomes differ from lysosomes?","choices":["Peroxisomes only exist within skeletal muscle cells, entirely absent from every other tissue type","Peroxisomes are formed by self-replication and contain oxidases (rather than acid hydrolases) to oxidize potentially poisonous substances like alcohol","Peroxisomes are considerably larger than the lysosomes and contain their own circular mitochondrial-like DNA","Peroxisomes have no surrounding membrane at all, existing as free enzyme clusters within the cytosol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Peroxisomes are physically similar to lysosomes but differ in two ways: they are formed by self-replication and contain oxidases rather than acid hydrolases, functioning to oxidize substances like alcohol that might otherwise be poisonous. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the function of autophagy?","choices":["A process for replicating nuclear DNA before mitosis, ensuring accurate duplication of every chromosome","A housekeeping process for degrading and recycling obsolete organelles and large protein aggregates, important for tissue development and cell survival when nutrients are scarce","A process for synthesizing entirely new proteins directly at the rough endoplasmic reticulum's ribosomes","A process for actively transporting ions across the plasma membrane against their own concentration gradient, using ATP directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Autophagy is a housekeeping process that degrades and recycles obsolete organelles and large protein aggregates, important for tissue development and cell survival during nutrient scarcity. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the primary function of mitochondria?","choices":["Extraction of energy from nutrients","Long-term storage of nuclear DNA","Synthesis of membrane phospholipids","Synthesis of new cytoplasmic proteins"],"answer":0,"exp":"The primary function of mitochondria is the extraction of energy from nutrients (ATP production). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which cytoskeletal element is composed of heterodimers of alpha and beta tubulin, making up spindle fibers and the core of the axoneme structure?","choices":["Microtubules","Thick filaments (myosin)","Intermediate filaments","Thin filaments (F-actin)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Microtubules are heterodimers of alpha and beta tubulin, forming spindle fibers and the core axoneme structure of cilia/flagella; intermediate filaments are cell-specific fibrillar monomers, thin filaments are F-actin, and thick filaments are myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the approximate functional diameter of a nuclear pore, and what is its approximate molecular weight permeability limit?","choices":["100 nm structural diameter, permeable to molecules of unlimited molecular weight","~1 nm functional diameter, impermeable to molecules above 500 MW","~9 nm functional diameter, permeable to molecules up to ~44,000 MW","9 nm structural diameter, permeable to proteins of any molecular weight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nuclear pores are 100 nm in diameter structurally, but their functional diameter is about 9 nm, selectively permeable to molecules up to about 44,000 MW. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the function of the nucleolus?","choices":["It is the primary site of cellular ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation","It stores tightly condensed DNA, in the form of chromatin, exclusively","It regulates the opening and closing of nuclear membrane ion channels","It contains RNA and proteins and functions to form the granular subunits of ribosomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nucleolus (one or more per nucleus, not membrane-delimited) contains RNA and proteins and functions to form the granular subunits of ribosomes; chromatin (condensed DNA) is found separately in the nucleoplasm. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where do molecules concentrate before receptor-mediated endocytosis proceeds?","choices":["Clathrin-coated pits on the cell surface","Cisternae within the Golgi apparatus","The matrix of peroxisomes","The interior of the nucleolus"],"answer":0,"exp":"In receptor-mediated endocytosis, molecules attach to cell-surface receptors concentrated in clathrin-coated pits; receptor binding induces invagination, and the process is ATP-dependent, involving actin and myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"According to the three-step overview of ATP production, what is the maximum number of ATP molecules formed per molecule of glucose degraded?","choices":["100","2","10","38"],"answer":3,"exp":"A maximum of 38 molecules of ATP are formed per molecule of glucose degraded, following the conversion to glucose/amino acids/fatty acids, processing into Acetyl-CoA, and reaction with O2. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What drives forward movement during ameboid locomotion?","choices":["Passive diffusion only, requiring no ATP hydrolysis or cytoskeletal proteins at all","Continual endocytosis at the tail and exocytosis at the leading edge, with forward movement from ATP-dependent interaction of actin and myosin","Osmotic pressure alone, drawing water into the leading edge in order to passively push the cell forward","Ongoing DNA replication occurring continuously within the moving cell's own nucleus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ameboid locomotion involves continual endocytosis at the \"tail\" and exocytosis at the leading edge (pseudopodium), with forward movement resulting from ATP-dependent interaction of actin and myosin. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the key functional difference between motile cilia and non-motile (primary) cilia?","choices":["Motile cilia require no ATP at all, moving instead through passive osmotic pressure changes","Motile cilia produce whiplike movements (e.g., respiratory airways, fallopian tubes); non-motile cilia occur singly per cell and serve as sensory antennae","Non-motile cilia are found only within the skeletal muscle fibres and are entirely absent everywhere else in the body","There is no functional difference at all; both types produce identical whiplike movements constantly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motile cilia produce whiplike movements mainly on surfaces like the respiratory airways and fallopian tubes (requiring ATP and Ca2+), while non-motile (primary) cilia generally occur as a single cilium per cell and serve as sensory antennae. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What are the three types of RNA involved in protein synthesis discussed in this lecture, and what is the role of mRNA specifically?","choices":["mRNA, tRNA, rRNA — mRNA is complementary to the DNA coding strand and organized into codons that each code for one amino acid","mRNA directly folds itself up into functional polypeptide chains, entirely without any translation step at all","mRNA is identical in sequence to the DNA coding strand, sharing thymine bases instead of uracil","Only tRNA is involved in protein synthesis, with no functional role for mRNA or rRNA"],"answer":0,"exp":"Messenger RNA (mRNA) is complementary in sequence to the DNA coding strand and organized in codons (triplet bases), with each codon coding for one amino acid (except AUG, the start codon specific for methionine). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"How does tRNA recognize which amino acid to deliver during translation?","choices":["By binding directly to the DNA coding strand within the nucleus during transcription","By purely random chance, with no sequence-specific recognition mechanism involved at all","Each tRNA combines with one amino acid and recognizes a specific mRNA codon via a complementary anticodon","tRNA does not interact with mRNA at all, instead binding ribosomal RNA directly for amino acid delivery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each transfer RNA (tRNA) acts as a carrier molecule, combining with one specific amino acid and recognizing a specific codon via a complementary anticodon on the tRNA molecule. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Approximately how genetically identical are humans at the genome level, according to this lecture?","choices":["100% identical with absolutely no variation across the entire genome","99.8% identical at the genome level, 99.999% identical in coding regions","50% identical across the entire coding and non-coding genome","75% identical across the entire coding and non-coding genome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Humans are approximately 99.8% identical at the genome level and 99.999% identical in the coding regions, despite having an estimated 25,000-30,000 genes. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What are the three phases of interphase (which makes up more than 95% of the cell life cycle)?","choices":["Prophase, metaphase, anaphase (phases of mitosis itself)","Mitosis, cytokinesis, and apoptosis (the cell death phases)","Transcription, translation, and replication (the molecular phases)","G1 phase, S phase (DNA synthesis), and G2 phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Interphase (>95% of the cell cycle) consists of the G1 phase, S phase (DNA synthesis), and G2 phase, distinct from the M phase (mitosis and cytokinesis). — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What forms on the lagging strand during DNA replication (S phase)?","choices":["Clathrin-coated pits","Ribosomal subunits","Okazaki fragments","Only continuous new DNA with no fragments"],"answer":2,"exp":"During S phase, DNA replication involves formation of Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand, with \"new\" DNA proofread by DNA polymerase and gaps filled by DNA ligase. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What is the role of DNA ligase in DNA repair?","choices":["It unwinds the DNA double helix ahead of the replication fork during synthesis","It replaces mismatches (cut out by DNA polymerase during proofreading) with complementary nucleotides","It forms new ribosomal subunits within the nucleolus during synthesis","It transcribes new mRNA strands directly from the DNA template"],"answer":1,"exp":"After DNA polymerase \"proofreads\" new DNA and cuts out mismatches, DNA ligase replaces the mismatches with complementary nucleotides; a mistake during transcription resulting in an abnormal protein is called a mutation. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What is the basis for cell differentiation, according to this lecture?","choices":["Permanent physical loss of unneeded genes as cells proliferate and specialize over time","Selective repression/expression of specific genes (not loss of genes), often via one part of the body inducing effects on another","A simple increase in mitochondrial number alone, as cells mature and specialize","Purely random mutation accumulating within the genome across many successive cell divisions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cell differentiation results not from the loss of genes but from the selective repression/expression of specific genes, with development occurring in large part through \"inductions\" — one part of the body affecting another. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which tissues are described as having rapid rates of cell growth/turnover?","choices":["Cardiac muscle and neurons only","Neurons and striated muscle","Bone marrow, skin, and intestinal epithelia","None — all tissues grow at the same rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bone marrow, skin, and intestinal epithelia show rapid cell growth, while smooth muscle, neurons, and striated muscle grow slowly or never; growth factors, contact inhibition, and negative-feedback cellular secretions determine growth rate. — Lecture 1 - Cell Physiology and Cell Membranes.pptx, Slide 105"}]};
